DTSTART:20260911T140000Z
DTEND:20260911T150000Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:Chornobyl Never Ends: 40 Years Later
DESCRIPTION:On April 26\, 1986\, the fourth reactor of the Chornobyl Nucl
 ear Power Plant exploded\, marking one of the largest technological and e
 nvironmental disasters of the twentieth century—a transnational\, epide
 miological\, environmental\, epistemological\, and historiographical rupt
 ure.  The event had far-reaching consequences for global health policy\, 
 nuclear energy development\, geopolitical power structures\, and our unde
 rstanding of knowledge\, technology\, and the environment. Yet Chornobyl 
 never ends.  Chornobyl Never Ends returns to the discourse and experience
  of Chornobyl forty years after the disaster\, looking beyond both the co
 llapse of the Soviet Union and the post-Soviet period to consider its con
 tinuing significance in the wake of four years of Russia's full-scale inv
 asion of Ukraine.  Oscillating between past\, present\, and future\, the 
 symposium asks what Chornobyl continues to mean—and what it makes visib
 le—four decades later. How has the disaster shaped our understanding of
  technology\, ecology\, risk\, memory\, and political power? How have its
  meanings changed across different historical moments and political conte
 xts? And what can the history of Chornobyl tell us about the uncertain nu
 clear futures ahead?  September 11\, 2026  10 a.m. – Keynote lecture:  
 "Communist Technology\, Rogue Operators\, and a Dysfunctional Industry: W
 as Chernobyl an Accident Waiting to Happen?"  Sonja Schmid (U.S. Nuclear 
 Regulatory Commission / Department of Science\, Technology and Society\, 
 Virginia Tech)  Lecture abstract:  The world's worst catastrophe at a nuc
 lear facility\, the 1986 Chornobyl disaster\, has alternately been blamed
  on a faulty reactor design\, incompetent reactor personnel\, or a deeply
  flawed economic system. This talk traces the extended "blame game" that 
 ensued the accident and its mitigation and shows that neither of these fa
 ctors alone caused the disaster. Rather\, it argues\, despite the general
  success of Soviet technology\, the country's outstanding engineering edu
 cation\, and a dynamic managerial culture\, Chornobyl was an accident of 
 history: both deeply Soviet\, and at the same time possible elsewhere.  S
 peaker's bio:  Sonja Schmid is a professor in the Department of Science\,
  Technology\, and Society at Virginia Tech (currently on leave)\, and the
  Historian at the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission. Her research is loc
 ated at the interface of national energy policies\, technological choices
 \, and nonproliferation concerns. Her first book\, Producing Power: The P
 re‑Chernobyl History of the Soviet Nuclear Industry\, was published by 
 MIT Press in 2015. Her next project focused on the challenges of globaliz
 ing nuclear emergency response\, and was supported by an NSF CAREER Award
 . In addition to teaching for and co-directing the STS graduate program i
 n Northern Virginia\, Dr. Schmid developed and supports a graduate certif
 icate\, "Nuclear Science\, Technology\, and Policy\," which is supported 
 by the nuclear engineering program and the policy school.  Location: GISB
  1060  11:30 a.m. – Film program:  "Nuclear Cinema: Imagining Anxiety\,
  Trauma\, and War"  Juggernaut (1968\, dir. Eugene Boyko) — 27 min  Ali
 ve Yet (1992\, dir. Heorhii Shkliarevskyi) — 28 min  Chornobyl 22 (2023
 \, dir. Oleksiy Radynski) — 22 min  Location: Wells Library Room 048 (I
 U Libraries Moving Image Archive Screening Room)  2:30 p.m. – Panel Dis
 cussion:  "Media\, Environment\, and Nuclear Security after Chornobyl"  P
 articipants:  ● Sonja Schmid (U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission / Virg
 inia Tech)  ● Andy Bruno (IU)  ● Stanislav Menzelevskyi (IU)  Moderat
 ion: Sarah Phillips (IU)  Location: GISB 4067  Cosponsored by The Byrnes 
 Institute (REEI)\, the Ukrainian Studies Organization\, the Moving Images
  Archive\, the Department of History\, and the Cohen Chair in Russian His
 tory.
