SUMMARY:IKS Colloquium - Sue Hyon Bae
DESCRIPTION:IKS Fall 2026 Colloquium Series  Sue Hyon Bae  Postdoctoral F
 ellow  Institute for Korean Studies  "Back to Nature: Traditional Medicin
 e and Cancer Cures"Friday\, September 1112:00-1:00pmGISB 2067 | 355 N Eag
 leson Ave  "South Korea has a long history of traditional medicine. Its f
 oundational text\, Hŏ Chun's Tongŭi Pogam\, published under royal edict
  in 1613\, is popularly cited today\, and basic concepts adapted from tra
 ditional medicine\, such as food as medicine and the superiority of local
  ingredients\, are commonly practiced along with biomedicine. In this pre
 sentation\, I analyze memoirs and self-help books that promote traditiona
 l Korean medicine (TKM) for the treatment of cancer. Written by diverse p
 eople\, some practitioners of TKM and others patients\, they share a comm
 on commitment to spreading an ideology\, or a narrative about how illness
  is created and cured.  Although the details such as what kind of natural
  cure and the degree of faith they place in biomedicine differ\, the TKM 
 narratives ultimately argue that there is something wrong with the state 
 of society. The TKM narratives also emphasize personal choice and control
 \, showing a unique syncretization of modern and traditional medicine wit
 h neoliberal values. Many people\, disillusioned not only with modern bio
 medicine but with modernity itself\, turn to alternatives just as they wo
 uld shop for different products in the belief they can buy health. What p
 eople blame for a disease that seems to have appeared out of nowhere\, wh
 at they believe will cure them\, and how traditional medicine is adapting
  itself to modernity even as it presents itself as a cure for modernity\,
  is highly revealing about contemporary South Korean attitudes towards he
 alth\, illness\, and modern life."  Open to the public. A light lunch wil
 l be served.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;">  IKS Fall 
 2026 Colloquium Series</h5><h6 style="text-align: center\;"><br />  Sue H
 yon Bae</h6><p style="text-align: center\;">  Postdoctoral Fellow<br />  
 Institute for Korean Studies</p><p style="text-align: center\;">  "Back t
 o Nature: Traditional Medicine and Cancer Cures"</p><p style="text-align:
  center\;"><strong>Friday\, September 11</strong></p><p style="text-align
 : center\;"><strong>12:00-1:00pm</strong></p><p style="text-align: center
 \;"><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-feature-clig-off font-
 feature-calt-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-none"><strong>GI
 SB 2067 | 355 N Eagleson Ave</strong><br /><br />  "South Korea has a lon
 g history of traditional medicine. Its foundational text\, Hŏ Chun's Ton
 gŭi Pogam\, published under royal edict in 1613\, is popularly cited tod
 ay\, and basic concepts adapted from traditional medicine\, such as food 
 as medicine and the superiority of local ingredients\, are commonly pract
 iced along with biomedicine. In this presentation\, I analyze memoirs and
  self-help books that promote traditional Korean medicine (TKM) for the t
 reatment of cancer. Written by diverse people\, some practitioners of TKM
  and others patients\, they share a common commitment to spreading an ide
 ology\, or a narrative about how illness is created and cured.<br />  Alt
 hough the details such as what kind of natural cure and the degree of fai
 th they place in biomedicine differ\, the TKM narratives ultimately argue
  that there is something wrong with the state of society. The TKM narrati
 ves also emphasize personal choice and control\, showing a unique syncret
 ization of modern and traditional medicine with neoliberal values. Many p
 eople\, disillusioned not only with modern biomedicine but with modernity
  itself\, turn to alternatives just as they would shop for different prod
 ucts in the belief they can buy health. What people blame for a disease t
 hat seems to have appeared out of nowhere\, what they believe will cure t
 hem\, and how traditional medicine is adapting itself to modernity even a
 s it presents itself as a cure for modernity\, is highly revealing about 
 contemporary South Korean attitudes towards health\, illness\, and modern
  life."<br /><br />  Open to the public. A light lunch will be served.</s
 pan></p>
UID:20260911T160000Z-2402247@events.iu.edu
