SUMMARY:American Foreign Policy in the Shadow of 9/11
DESCRIPTION:Twenty-five years ago on 9/11\, al-Qaeda terrorists attacked 
 the United States\, inflicting a death toll greater than Japan's surprise
  attack on Pearl Harbor. The attacks delivered a profound shock to Americ
 a's sense of security and place in the world and the international system
 . In response\, the United States undertook a far-reaching "War on Terror
 "—the aftermath of which continues to influence our politics\, national
  security\, and the global balance of power. Counterterrorism\, once a pe
 ripheral issue\, quickly became the dominant policy priority. Washington 
 launched large-scale and prolonged military interventions in Afghanistan\
 , Iraq\, and other crisis hotspots. Longstanding alliances were tested as
  new partnerships emerged. New forms of unmanned warfare were pioneered w
 hile national security agencies ballooned in size\, budgets\, and legal a
 uthorities. In short\, 9/11 transformed American statecraft.  Join Profes
 sor of Practice Zachary Constantino for a moderated conversation with U.S
 . Ambassador (retired) Michael McKinley to unpack the wide-ranging impact
  the September 11 attacks had on American foreign policy. This will be an
  afternoon of reflection\, assessing two decades of U.S. strategy and the
  legacy of those policies. Ambassador McKinley served for thirty-seven ye
 ars in the U.S. Foreign Service\, including as Ambassador to Afghanistan\
 , Brazil\, Peru\, and Colombia. He also held senior diplomatic posts in E
 urope\, Africa\, and Washington\, DC. Ambassador McKinley's experience on
  the front lines of diplomacy is extensive\, encompassing efforts to adva
 nce peace negotiations in Colombia and Afghanistan to end conflict as wel
 l as successfully negotiating agreements to promote U.S. investment and m
 arket access. He is currently a senior counselor at The Cohen Group and a
  non-resident senior advisor with the Center for Strategic and Internatio
 nal Studies. Ambassador McKinley is the author of numerous articles on fo
 reign policy that have appeared in Foreign Affairs\, Politico\, The&#160\
 ;Washington Post\, and other publications.  The IU Chapter of the Alexand
 er Hamilton Society is co-sponsoring this event.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Twenty-five years ago on 9/11\, al-Qaed
 a terrorists attacked the United States\, inflicting a death toll greater
  than Japan's surprise attack on Pearl Harbor. The attacks delivered a pr
 ofound shock to America's sense of security and place in the world and th
 e international system. In response\, the United States undertook a far-r
 eaching "War on Terror"—the aftermath of which continues to influence o
 ur politics\, national security\, and the global balance of power. Counte
 rterrorism\, once a peripheral issue\, quickly became the dominant policy
  priority. Washington launched large-scale and prolonged military interve
 ntions in Afghanistan\, Iraq\, and other crisis hotspots. Longstanding al
 liances were tested as new partnerships emerged. New forms of unmanned wa
 rfare were pioneered while national security agencies ballooned in size\,
  budgets\, and legal authorities. In short\, 9/11 transformed American st
 atecraft.</p><p>  Join Professor of Practice Zachary Constantino for a mo
 derated conversation with U.S. Ambassador (retired) Michael McKinley to u
 npack the wide-ranging impact the September 11 attacks had on American fo
 reign policy. This will be an afternoon of reflection\, assessing two dec
 ades of U.S. strategy and the legacy of those policies. Ambassador McKinl
 ey served for thirty-seven years in the U.S. Foreign Service\, including 
 as Ambassador to Afghanistan\, Brazil\, Peru\, and Colombia. He also held
  senior diplomatic posts in Europe\, Africa\, and Washington\, DC. Ambass
 ador McKinley's experience on the front lines of diplomacy is extensive\,
  encompassing efforts to advance peace negotiations in Colombia and Afgha
 nistan to end conflict as well as successfully negotiating agreements to 
 promote U.S. investment and market access. He is currently a senior couns
 elor at The Cohen Group and a non-resident senior advisor with the Center
  for Strategic and International Studies. Ambassador McKinley is the auth
 or of numerous articles on foreign policy that have appeared in <em>Forei
 gn Affairs</em>\, <em>Politico</em>\, <em>The&#160\;Washington Post</em>\
 , and other publications.</p><p>  The IU Chapter of the Alexander Hamilto
 n Society is co-sponsoring this event.</p>
