DTSTART:20260918T160000Z
DTEND:20260918T180000Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
SUMMARY:Tobias Center Research Seminar: Prolonged Harm and Legacies of Vi
 olence
DESCRIPTION:This installment of the Tobias Center research seminar series
 \, entitled "Prolonged Harm and Legacies of Violence\," features Rana Kho
 ury\, Assistant Professor of Political Science at the University of Illin
 ois Urbana-Champaign.Abstract: Most refugees live in protracted displacem
 ent for years without achieving permanent rights. Civilians live through 
 wars themselves protracted. How do war's victims think and behave when ci
 rcumstances change? Conflict legacies scholarship conceptualizes violence
  as discrete exposure and examines its effects on victims after political
  conditions have stabilized. These approaches overlook prolonged harm: cu
 mulative\, mundane\, and ongoing hardship that persists beyond episodic v
 iolence and shapes how individuals experience change itself. Prolonged ha
 rm cumulates across multiple dimensions: psychological\, structural\, mat
 erial\, and emotional. A proposed conceptual framework captures how Syria
 n refugees navigated two critical but highly distinct moments late in the
 ir displacement—natural disaster and regime change. Both activated and 
 exposed the cumulated harm of conflict victims. Findings suggest conflict
  research must account for quotidian hardship that endogenously imprints 
 conflict legacies. Differing methodological traditions can incorporate a 
 prolonged harm framework.Lunch will be provided.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This installment of the Tobias Center r
 esearch seminar series\, entitled "Prolonged Harm and Legacies of Violenc
 e\," features Rana Khoury\, Assistant Professor of Political Science at t
 he University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign.</p><p><strong>Abstract:</stro
 ng> Most refugees live in protracted displacement for years without achie
 ving permanent rights. Civilians live through wars themselves protracted.
  How do war's victims think and behave when circumstances change? Conflic
 t legacies scholarship conceptualizes violence as discrete exposure and e
 xamines its effects on victims after political conditions have stabilized
 . These approaches overlook prolonged harm: cumulative\, mundane\, and on
 going hardship that persists beyond episodic violence and shapes how indi
 viduals experience change itself. Prolonged harm cumulates across multipl
 e dimensions: psychological\, structural\, material\, and emotional. A pr
 oposed conceptual framework captures how Syrian refugees navigated two cr
 itical but highly distinct moments late in their displacement—natural d
 isaster and regime change. Both activated and exposed the cumulated harm 
 of conflict victims. Findings suggest conflict research must account for 
 quotidian hardship that endogenously imprints conflict legacies. Differin
 g methodological traditions can incorporate a prolonged harm framework.</
 p><p><strong>Lunch will be provided.</strong></p>
