 rev.1755779713.png 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="300" height=
 "387" alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://events.iu.ed
 u/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/387/27715_paredes.rev.1755779713.pn
 g" data-max-w="471" data-max-h="607" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"
 /></picture></p><p><strong>Bio</strong></p><p>  Alyssa Paredes is an anth
 ropologist with interests in industrial agriculture\, transnational suppl
 y chains\, and environmental litigation between the Philippines and Japan
 . Her work appears in journals in anthropology\, history\, geography\, fo
 od studies\, and Asian studies. Her book\, <em>Bananapocalypse: Plantatio
 n Capitalism and the Unmaking of Asia's Banana Republic</em>\, will be pu
 blished by the University of California Press in 2026. She is also co-edi
 tor of <em>Halo-Halo Ecologies: The Emergent Environments Behind Filipino
  Food</em> (University of Hawaii Press 2025).</p><p><strong>Title</strong
 ></p><p>  Air-conditioned People and their Others: Class and Environmenta
 l Litigation in the Philippines</p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></p><p>  I
 n the Philippines\, <em>naka-aircon</em> or "air-conditioned person" is a
  pejorative phrase describing someone as apathetic or detached from reali
 ty. It captures classed inequalities in access to hermetically sealed env
 ironments protected from the natural world. In Davao City\, activists eng
 aged in legal battles over chemical drift use the phrase to explain how t
 he indifference of the elite dealt a fatal blow to their court case. This
  essay uses the idiomatic expression to argue that classed disparities in
  the experience of the atmospheric uncommons bear repercussions for the r
 ule of law. While scholarship has considered how environmental disparitie
 s are the <em>result</em> of state weakness\, this work considers how it 
 may be the <em>root</em> of state inertia as well. Environmental equity\,
  then\, is not only a goal of legal equity\, but instead its prerequisite
 . The essay also invites reflection on the air-conditioned condition of f
 ield-based research in which the anthropologist\, too\, is uninnocent.</p
 ><p><strong><span data-contrast="none">About the Colloquium Series</span>
 </strong></p><p><span data-contrast="none">Each Monday throughout the aca
 demic year\, speakers share and discuss cutting edge research echoing the
  themes of the Ostrom Workshop's research program. Topics range from the 
 governance of space\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to the enviro
 nment\, political economy\, and sustainable food science. Presenters are 
 from IU and around the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zoom to lea
 rn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today. For more information\, see ou
 r</span><span data-contrast="none"><a class="external" href="https://ostr
 omworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">website
 </a></span><span data-contrast="none">.</span></p> Meeting URL: https://i
 u.zoom.us/j/87671999280
