GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
SUMMARY:Lecture: "Polish–South Netherlandish Humanist Networks during t
 he 16th and 17th Centuries" with Michał Czerenkiewicz
DESCRIPTION:The mutual humanistic connections between the Polish-Lithuani
 an Commonwealth and the Southern Low Countries embraced\, among others\, 
 the academic life and literary production. The networks formed by the hum
 anists and the students who traveled to the University of Leuven are a cl
 ear example of the vivid role of the Latin literary culture.  Michał Cze
 renkiewicz is an assistant professor at the Faculty of Polish Studies at 
 the Jagiellonian University in Kraków. His scientific work focuses on ea
 rly modern book history\, Neo-Latin literature\, and Polish-South Netherl
 andish connections\, mainly in the 17th century. He authored three books 
 and is an editorial board member of the "Terminus" journal.  Cosponsored 
 by the Polish Studies Center\, The Byrnes Insitutes (REEI)\, and the Inst
 itute for European Studies\,&#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The mutual humanistic connections betwe
 en the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth and the Southern Low Countries embr
 aced\, among others\, the academic life and literary production. The netw
 orks formed by the humanists and the students who traveled to the Univers
 ity of Leuven are a clear example of the vivid role of the Latin literary
  culture.</p><p>  Michał Czerenkiewicz is an assistant professor at the 
 Faculty of Polish Studies at the Jagiellonian University in Kraków. His 
 scientific work focuses on early modern book history\, Neo-Latin literatu
 re\, and Polish-South Netherlandish connections\, mainly in the 17th cent
 ury. He authored three books and is an editorial board member of the "Ter
 minus" journal.</p><p>  Cosponsored by the Polish Studies Center\, The By
 rnes Insitutes (REEI)\, and the Institute for European Studies\,&#160\;</
 p>
UID:20260925T180000Z-2402262@events.iu.edu
