SUMMARY:21JPSI Special Event: "The U.S.-Japan Alliance and the Free and O
 pen Indo-Pacific" (Minister ODAIRA (Japan's Ministry of Foreign Affairs))
DESCRIPTION:Minister Odaira's prepared remarks will be followed by a Q&am
 p\;A/discussion moderated by 21JPSI Director Adam P. Liff. **Please note 
 that this event will be held under the Chatham House rule (no recording\,
  attribution\, or quoting\, please). (https://www.chathamhouse.org/about-
 us/chatham-house-rule) .**&#160\;  In his first public talk on the subjec
 t\, Minister for Public Affairs Masatsugu Odaira (Embassy of Japan in Was
 hington\, DC) will provide his personal assessment of the current and fut
 ure of U.S.-Japan relations and discuss Japan's Free and Open Indo-Pacifi
 c (FOIP) vision–which Prime Minister Takaichi updated in a major speech
  in Vietnam this past spring. Against the backdrop of "an increasingly se
 vere international environment" and intensifying geopolitical competition
 \, Japan's updated FOIP framework expands Tokyo's vision of maritime secu
 rity\, digital connectivity\, and economic resilience. It is designed to 
 foster multi-ocean collaboration between East Asia\, ASEAN\, South Asia\,
  the Pacific\, and coastal Africa to ensure regional stability.&#160\;Thi
 s event is open to all students\, faculty\, staff\, and members of the co
 mmunity.&#160\;About the speaker:Mr. Masatsugu ODAIRA\, Minister of Publi
 c Affairs at the Embassy of Japan in the United States of America\, assum
 ed his present position in September 2025.  Minister Odaira joined the Mi
 nistry of Foreign Affairs in 1998 after graduating from Tokyo University'
 s Faculty of Law\, and has since served mainly in the areas of China affa
 irs and International Law. He most recently served as Director of the Fir
 st China and Mongolia Division (2024-25) and as Director of the Internati
 onal Legal Affairs Division (2021-24).  He has also worked in Japan-US af
 fairs\, in both economic and security matters\, serving in the North Amer
 ican Affairs Bureau (2008-10\, 2012-13). Additionally\, while serving as 
 Deputy Director in the National Security Secretariat (2014)\, he worked o
 n legal issues in Japanese security policy regarding the Japanese Constit
 ution in the context of the Right of Self-Defense.  He has served as Exec
 utive Assistant to the Minister for Foreign Affairs (2010-12) and to the 
 Vice-Minister for Foreign Affairs (2018-21). He has also served abroad in
  China as Counsellor (Political Section) of the Embassy of Japan in the P
 eople's Republic of China (2014-17).Prof. Adam LIFF (host/moderator) is P
 rofessor of East Asian International Relations at Indiana University's Ha
 milton Lugar School of Global and International Studies\, where he also s
 erves as Founding Director of its 21st Century Japan Politics and Society
  Initiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Senior Fellow wit
 h the Center for Asia Policy Studies at the Brookings Institution\, as we
 ll as a Faculty Associate and Associate-in-Research at Harvard University
 's Program on U.S.-Japan Relations and Reischauer Institute of Japanese S
 tudies. His research examines contemporary international security and for
 eign policy challenges in East Asia—with a particular focus on Japanese
  politics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Japan security alliance\; U.S. al
 lies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relat
 ions. He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics from Princeton University\, a
  postgraduate research certificate in International Politics from the Uni
 versity of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian Studies and Psychology from S
 tanford University.  &#160\;Unless otherwise noted\, all 21JPSI events ar
 e 100% in-person.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em>Minister Odaira's prepared remarks wi
 ll be followed by a Q&amp\;A/discussion moderated by 21JPSI Director Adam
  P. Liff. <strong>**Please note that this event will be held under <a hre
 f="https://www.chathamhouse.org/about-us/chatham-house-rule" target="_bla
 nk" rel="noopener">the Chatham House rule (no recording\, attribution\, o
 r quoting\, please).</a> .**&#160\;</strong></em></p><p>  In his first pu
 blic talk on the subject\, <strong>Minister for Public Affairs Masatsugu 
 Odaira</strong> (Embassy of Japan in Washington\, DC) will provide his pe
 rsonal assessment of the current and future of U.S.-Japan relations and d
 iscuss Japan's Free and Open Indo-Pacific (FOIP) vision–which Prime Min
 ister Takaichi updated in a major speech in Vietnam this past spring. Aga
 inst the backdrop of "an increasingly severe international environment" a
 nd intensifying geopolitical competition\, Japan's updated FOIP framework
  expands Tokyo's vision of maritime security\, digital connectivity\, and
  economic resilience. It is designed to foster multi-ocean collaboration 
 between East Asia\, ASEAN\, South Asia\, the Pacific\, and coastal Africa
  to ensure regional stability.&#160\;</p><p><em>This event is open to all
  students\, faculty\, staff\, and members of the community.&#160\;</em></
 p><p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline\;">About the speaker
 :</span></strong></p><p><strong>Mr. Masatsugu ODAIRA\,</strong> Minister 
 of Public Affairs at the Embassy of Japan in the United States of America
 \, assumed his present position in September 2025.</p><p>  Minister Odair
 a joined the Ministry of Foreign Affairs in 1998 after graduating from To
 kyo University's Faculty of Law\, and has since served mainly in the area
 s of China affairs and International Law. He most recently served as Dire
 ctor of the First China and Mongolia Division (2024-25) and as Director o
 f the International Legal Affairs Division (2021-24).</p><p>  He has also
  worked in Japan-US affairs\, in both economic and security matters\, ser
 ving in the North American Affairs Bureau (2008-10\, 2012-13). Additional
 ly\, while serving as Deputy Director in the National Security Secretaria
 t (2014)\, he worked on legal issues in Japanese security policy regardin
 g the Japanese Constitution in the context of the Right of Self-Defense.<
 /p><p>  He has served as Executive Assistant to the Minister for Foreign 
 Affairs (2010-12) and to the Vice-Minister for Foreign Affairs (2018-21).
  He has also served abroad in China as Counsellor (Political Section) of 
 the Embassy of Japan in the People's Republic of China (2014-17).</p><p><
 strong>Prof. Adam LIFF (host/moderator)</strong> is Professor of East Asi
 an International Relations at Indiana University's Hamilton Lugar School 
 of Global and International Studies\, where he also serves as Founding Di
 rector of its 21st Century Japan Politics and Society Initiative ("21JPSI
 "). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Senior Fellow with the Center for Asi
 a Policy Studies at the Brookings Institution\, as well as a Faculty Asso
 ciate and Associate-in-Research at Harvard University's Program on U.S.-J
 apan Relations and Reischauer Institute of Japanese Studies. His research
  examines contemporary international security and foreign policy challeng
 es in East Asia—with a particular focus on Japanese politics and foreig
 n policy\; the U.S.-Japan security alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.
 S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.
 D. and M.A. in Politics from Princeton University\, a postgraduate resear
 ch certificate in International Politics from the University of Tokyo\, a
 nd a B.A. in East Asian Studies and Psychology from Stanford University.<
 /p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><em>Unless otherwise noted\, all 21JPSI events are 
 100% in-person.</em></p>
UID:20261027T200000Z-2402235@events.iu.edu
