LOCATION:HODGE HALL (KELLEY SCHOOL OF BUSINESS)
GEO:39.172366;-86.518375
SUMMARY:21JPSI Speaker Series "The Future of Space Security in Asia" (Pro
 f. Saadia Pekkanen (University of Washington))
DESCRIPTION:A public talk/Q&amp\;A about space security in Asia by one of
  the world's leading experts. Hodge Hall (HH) 2079&#160\;(corner of E 10t
 h St and N Fee Lane)  This public event is open to all students\, faculty
 \, staff\, and members of the community.Please join us!Space has emerged 
 as a consequential arena of strategic competition\, defense\, and conflic
 t. Asia\, with the greatest concentration of the world's top independent 
 space powers\, is at the forefront of these dynamics. Japan's growing cap
 abilities\, changing operational postures\, and deepening space collabora
 tion with the United States are contributing to – and helping shape –
  this evolving strategic environment\, and broader prospects for war and 
 peace.This event is co-sponsored by IU's Cybersecurity and Global Policy 
 Program\, the Department of Political Science\, and the Space Governance 
 Lab.  ————————————Saadia M. Pekkanen is the Job a
 nd Gertrud Tamaki Endowed Professor of International Studies\, Adjunct Pr
 ofessor of Political Science\, and Adjunct Professor of Law at the Univer
 sity of Washington in Seattle. She is founding director of the Program on
  Strategy\, Policy\, and Diplomacy Research (SPDR) at The Henry M. Jackso
 n School of International Studies and the Founding Director of the Space 
 Law\, Data\, and Policy Program (SPACE LDP) at the School of Law. Her res
 earch interests are in outer space security\, diplomacy\, and internation
 al affairs\, with a focus on Japan and Asia. She has published widely\, a
 nd is most recently co-editor of the special issue on Space Diplomacy: Th
 e Final Frontier of Theory and Practice\, and co-editor of The Oxford Han
 dbook of Space Security. She earned Master's degrees from Columbia Univer
 sity and Yale Law School\, and a doctorate from Harvard University in pol
 itical science. She is an elected member of the International Institute o
 f Space Law (IISL) and a lifetime member of the Council of Foreign Relati
 ons (CFR). For further information please visit\, saadiapekkanen.com.Adam
  LIFF (host/moderator) is Professor of East Asian International Relations
  at Indiana University's Hamilton Lugar School of Global and Internationa
 l Studies\, where he also serves as Founding Director of its 21st Century
  Japan Politics and Society Initiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a No
 nresident Senior Fellow with the Center for Asia Policy Studies at the Br
 ookings Institution\, as well as a Faculty Associate and Associate-in-Res
 earch at Harvard University's Program on U.S.-Japan Relations and Reischa
 uer Institute of Japanese Studies.&#160\;His research examines contempora
 ry international security and foreign policy challenges in East Asia—wi
 th a particular focus on Japanese politics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-
 Japan security alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific stra
 tegy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Polit
 ics from Princeton University\, a postgraduate research certificate in In
 ternational Politics from the University of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East As
 ian Studies and Psychology from Stanford University&#160\;All 21JPSI even
 ts are 100% in-person unless otherwise noted. This event is made possible
  thanks to generous support from the Japan Foundation.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div id="lw_cal_event_detail_intro" class="l
 w_cal_event_detail_block"><p>    A public talk/Q&amp\;A about space secur
 ity in Asia by one of the world's leading experts. <strong>Hodge Hall (HH
 ) 2079&#160\;</strong>(corner of E 10th St and N Fee Lane)  </p></div><di
 v id="lw_cal_event_detail_description" class="lw_cal_event_detail_block">
 <p><em>This public event is open to all students\, faculty\, staff\, and 
 members of the community.</em><em>Please join us!</em></p><p><span data-o
 lk-copy-source="MailCompose">Space has emerged as a consequential arena o
 f strategic competition\, defense\, and conflict. Asia\, with the greates
 t concentration of the world's top independent space powers\, is at the f
 orefront of these dynamics. Japan's growing capabilities\, changing opera
 tional postures\, and deepening space collaboration with the United State
 s are contributing to – and helping shape – this evolving strategic e
 nvironment\, and broader prospects for war and peace.</span></p><p><span 
 data-olk-copy-source="MailCompose"><em>This event is co-sponsored by IU's
  Cybersecurity and Global Policy Program\, the Department of Political Sc
 ience\, and the Space Governance Lab.</em></span></p></div><p>  ———
 —————————</p><div class="profile-about"><div itemprop="
 description articleSection"><p><span data-olk-copy-source="MailCompose"><
 strong>Saadia M. Pekkanen</strong> is the Job and Gertrud Tamaki Endowed 
 Professor of International Studies\, Adjunct Professor of Political Scien
 ce\, and Adjunct Professor of Law at the University of Washington in Seat
 tle. She is founding director of the Program on Strategy\, Policy\, and D
 iplomacy Research (SPDR) at The Henry M. Jackson School of International 
 Studies and the Founding Director of the Space Law\, Data\, and Policy Pr
 ogram (SPACE LDP) at the School of Law. Her research interests are in out
 er space security\, diplomacy\, and international affairs\, with a focus 
 on Japan and Asia. She has published widely\, and is most recently co-edi
 tor of the special issue on <i>Space Diplomacy: The Final Frontier of The
 ory and Practice</i>\, and co-editor of <i>The Oxford Handbook of Space S
 ecurity</i>. She earned Master's degrees from Columbia University and Yal
 e Law School\, and a doctorate from Harvard University in political scien
 ce. She is an elected member of the International Institute of Space Law 
 (IISL) and a lifetime member of the Council of Foreign Relations (CFR). F
 or further information please visit\, saadiapekkanen.com.</span></p></div
 ></div><p><strong>Adam LIFF (host/moderator)</strong> is Professor of Eas
 t Asian International Relations at Indiana University's Hamilton Lugar Sc
 hool of Global and International Studies\, where he also serves as Foundi
 ng Director of its 21st Century Japan Politics and Society Initiative ("2
 1JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Senior Fellow with the Center fo
 r Asia Policy Studies at the Brookings Institution\, as well as a Faculty
  Associate and Associate-in-Research at Harvard University's Program on U
 .S.-Japan Relations and Reischauer Institute of Japanese Studies.&#160\;H
 is research examines contemporary international security and foreign poli
 cy challenges in East Asia—with a particular focus on Japanese politics
  and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Japan security alliance\; U.S. allies and 
 Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations. He 
 holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics from Princeton University\, a postgrad
 uate research certificate in International Politics from the University o
 f Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian Studies and Psychology from Stanford U
 niversity&#160\;</p><p><em>All 21JPSI events are 100% in-person unless ot
 herwise noted. This event is made possible thanks to generous support fro
 m the Japan Foundation.</em></p>
